A||||5||Ferrari_cdn||blinking headlight||||1||
Z||000000||Ferrari_cdn||11-11-2001||11:03 PM||||My Fiero is solid but seemingly have quite a few little issues :P<P>Anyway my headlights are working properly, except sometimes when I turn them on, both lights come on and going up.  About 1 second later, the left headlight will go off, then on again another second later.  Sometimes, it will stay off.  I'll just turn off and on and it'll come back on.  The headlight motors are working properly, always going up or down.  Just the light itself, sometimes blinks, sometimes stays.  I've said she likes to wink at other cars when starting up   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  But this is just weird.  If it did blink, it'll stay on until I turn it off.  I think it might be a going relay but what do you think?<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ferrari_cdn" TARGET=_blank>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_cdn.JPG">   </A>  <BR>AIM: CanadianFiero<p>[This message has been edited by Ferrari_cdn (edited 11-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Jncomutt||11-11-2001||11:11 PM||||You might be able to eliminate some possibilities with a quick visual check.  <P>Are the connections loose or corroded/rusted?<P>Are the wires cut, or showin any wear?<BR>I dunnoo too much about the relay, but my light did similar and it was a cut wire..  It got pulled when the head light motor went up, sometimees its make connectioon, sometimes pulled apart..  Just a quick opinion.||||reg||1
Z||000002||edbone||11-11-2001||11:33 PM||||You may just have to replace that light. The philament inside may just be broken and hanging,and any vibration or movent may be just be enough for it to make contact and stay on untill you shut her down.(I think I spelled philament right)||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-12-2001||12:34 AM||||It won't be a relay or any thing like that...<P>As ed said... bad lamp<BR>or<BR>bad socket<BR>or<BR>bad ground<P>see if it does it in high beam.... If it's only one beam then its the lamp or socket.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ferrari_cdn||11-12-2001||01:21 AM||||High beam works just fine.<P>Maybe I'll try switching the lights, and see if the right one blinks.||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||11-12-2001||12:50 PM||||It's a bad lamp or one terminal of the socket.<P>Just get new lamps. It's not worth the work to swap them. (There's a faster way to change them in my cave...) 2 new lamps are cheap. even cheaper if you can get them onsale at K/Wall mart.<P>The filament can break like mentioned above but it usually only welds itself once or twice before quitting entirely. I'm guessing one of the terminals on the back of the lamp has cracked loose. It's a common problem.<P>Don't waste time trying to solder on a cracked lamp terminal. Regular solder isn't strong enough and will likely crack off again in short order. If it a plastic shell lamp the thing will probly melt which ruins the lamp focus. A glas shell could crack from the uneven heating of home soldering.<P>If a new lamp has the same problem then on wire on the socket has probably broken inside the insulation right at the terminal. Not hard to fix that either.<P>Always replace lamps as pairs. You'll get much better results, especially with the "high visiblity" ones from Philips and others. I save the good old lamp in case one gets whacked with a rock or something.||||reg||1
